>
	<p>
		Hi, I’m Sanvid Maniyar. I’m a software and solutions architect based in Adelaide, Australia,
		with close to two decades of experience designing, building and integrating enterprise systems.
	</p>

	<p>
		My background is primarily in the Microsoft and Azure ecosystem, including .NET, Azure App
		Service, Azure Functions, API Management, SQL Server, Azure DevOps, Key Vault, Application
		Insights, Service Bus and broader integration architecture. Much of my work sits at the point
		where applications, APIs, data and external platforms need to work together reliably.
	</p>

	<h2>What I’m exploring now</h2>
	<p>
		I’m increasingly focused on automation and AI-assisted software delivery. That includes n8n,
		Power Automate, AI agents, model integration, developer tooling and ways to connect AI systems
		with real business processes rather than treating AI as an isolated demo.
	</p>

	<p>
		I also enjoy experimenting with self-hosted infrastructure, containers, Cloudflare Workers,
		Docker, Portainer and small home-lab projects. Maniyars Web is one of those experiments: a place
		to learn in public, document what works and turn useful technical discoveries into reusable notes.
	</p>

	<h2>What you’ll find here</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Practical Azure architecture and integration patterns</li>
		<li>API Management, networking, security and observability notes</li>
		<li>n8n and workflow automation experiments</li>
		<li>AI agents and applied AI integration ideas</li>
		<li>Cloudflare, Docker and self-hosting projects</li>
		<li>Lessons learned from solving real engineering problems</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Why this site exists</h2>
	<p>
		Technology changes quickly, and the most useful knowledge is often discovered while solving a
		real problem. I created this site to keep those lessons organised, improve my own understanding
		and share solutions that may save another developer or architect some time.
	</p>

	<p>
		The goal is simple: practical engineering, continuous learning and useful technology without
		unnecessary complexity.
	</p>
</Layout>
